/target
models/
*.onnx
# Fixture models are stored as git-friendly ONNX protobuf TextFormat
# (`*.onnx.textproto`), which is not matched by the `*.onnx` ignore above and is
# therefore tracked. The line below documents that intent explicitly.
!**/*.onnx.textproto
# No checked-in graph fixture is exempt from the TextFormat policy. Generators
# may materialize binary ONNX files locally, but those files remain ignored.
*.safetensors
.DS_Store

# Rust
# Existing Rust entries above: /target and Cargo.lock (left as-is).
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.Python
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.so
.coverage
htmlcov/
.tox/

# Squad: local-backend runtime state. These slash-separated patterns work in
# Git's path syntax on Windows and POSIX. Static team/config/charter/template
# files remain tracked; the inbox README is its intentional placeholder.
.squad/decisions/inbox/*
!.squad/decisions/inbox/README.md
.squad/agents/*/history*.md
.squad/orchestration-log/
.squad/log/
.squad/sessions/
.squad/identity/now.md
.squad/memory/
.squad/.scratch/
.squad/.cache/
# Squad: SubSquad activation file (local to this machine)
.squad-workstream

# Timeline written by `/profile on` when no destination is named. The curated
# samples under examples/traces/ are committed deliberately and are not this.
onnx-genai-session.perfetto.json

# Scratch files used to pass long commit messages to git commit -F.
.msg.txt
.pr-body.md
.git-msg.txt
# Belt and braces: the convention above is easy to forget, and a stray commit-
# message file has reached a PR before.
CM*.txt
PR*.md
# Extensionless variant. Removed once by 490b846c3 (as a .commitmsg/ directory)
# and re-committed as a file by #1881, because deleting the artifact left the
# name matching none of the patterns above and nothing stopped the next one.
# Root-level only, per /*.log below: it is only ever created where you run
# `git commit -F`, so a nested file of that name is somebody's, not this.
/.commitmsg
# Issue and review bodies staged the same way, for gh issue create --body-file.
.issue-*.md
# Shorter variant of .pr-body.md above. Reached main in #2026 as the fourth file
# of this class, hours after two separate fixes to this very block. Root-level
# only, per /*.log below. This line is the trailing half of that repair: see
# .github/root-file-allowlist.txt for the half that does not have to guess what
# the next one will be called.
/.body.md
# Per-agent scratch directories: .roy-scratch/pr.md reached main in #2150 as the
# fifth artefact of this class, and the first one a directory since 490b846c3.
# The wildcard is the guessing half of the repair -- the next one will be named
# by whoever creates it, and only .github/root-file-allowlist.txt is able to
# catch a name nobody predicted. Root-level only, per /*.log above.
/.*-scratch/
# Review bodies and merge-gate logs, written a directory down rather than at the
# root. `.review/` is not a `-scratch` name, so the wildcard above does not reach
# it, and `.review/pr.log` is not caught by `/*.log` below either: that pattern is
# anchored to the root, so a .log one directory down matches nothing. Verified
# with `git check-ignore -v .review/pr.log` (no match) against root `pr.log`
# (matches /*.log). Found untracked in two worktrees before it was committed --
# the allowlist check would have caught it, but only after everyone had inherited
# the red.
/.review/
# Scratch buffers used when moving a block of code between files.
.block.txt
# Test/build output teed to a file while investigating. Root-level only, so a
# fixture or a crate that legitimately tracks a .log is unaffected. Added after
# a 1364-line `cargo test` transcript reached main in #1951: the file was named
# outside every pattern above, and `git add -A` does not care what a file is
# for.
/*.log

# seq-major microbenchmark scratch crate
/bench-seqmajor/target/
bench-bins/

# Per-crate scratch written by tests at runtime (e.g. the weight-handle fixtures
# in onnx-runtime-session). Only /target is ignored above, so these nested dirs
# would otherwise be picked up by a `git add -A crates/`.
crates/*/target/

# Sebastian marlin-bench scratch: re-exported ORT-genai glm package + staging (large, reproducible)
/ort-glm-build/
/ort-glm-stage/
/ort-glm-cache/
/bench-logs/

# Roy's local benchmark scratch (see docs/benchmarks/*): raw arm dumps and
# machine-specific binary paths, never repo content.
.rg_*
.rv_*
.roy_*
# Hyphenated variant. `.roy_*` does not match `.roy-scratch/`, so a scratch dir
# named with a hyphen was committed by `add -A` and survived two reviews.
.roy-*
# Summary transcript from roy_validate.sh. The per-gate logs match `.rv_*`,
# but the summary did not, so it was easy to commit by accident with `add -A`.
roy_validate.log

# Deckard local bench env + scratch (not for PR)
.deckard_env.sh
.scratch/

# hostlock self-test scratch (scripts/hostlock_test.sh writes these into the repo
# root deliberately -- /tmp is off-limits here -- and an aborted run leaves them)
.hostlock-selftest*
.scratch-paged/

# Leon's PR-prep scratch: PR bodies, review responses, merge-gate logs and
# mutation-battery drivers, written into a worktree root because /tmp is
# off-limits here. `.body.md` reached main this way in #2026 -- `git add -A`
# does not distinguish a dotfile at the root from repo content, and a name
# like `.body.md` looks plausible enough in `git status` to skim past.
.leon_*
.body.md
.resp*.md
.prbody*.md
# Root-anchored: these four are ordinary nouns, and a nested crate or fixture
# could reasonably want one. The scratch only ever lands in a worktree root.
/.merge.log
/.mutate*
/.suite.log
/.selftest.log

# Local-only working directories. These are not "files someone might add by
# accident" but directories every agent on a shared box creates by convention:
# throwaway worktrees for validating a PR against latest main, and a vendored
# upstream checkout. Measured before adding: `git add -A` in a primary checkout
# staged 69 files from .validation-worktrees alone. The root-file allowlist job
# does catch this once committed -- it reports the root segment, not the leaf --
# so this is the layer that stops it reaching a commit at all, not a substitute.
# Root-anchored, because a crate could legitimately hold a nested fixture dir of
# any of these names and only the repository root is scratch.
/.validation-worktrees/
/.worktrees/
/.ort_upstream/
